# Incident review template

Copy this into the incident's issue and fill it in. The point is not to find a person but to find **which layer should have caught it and didn't**, and to put the fix into that layer.

## What happened

- Symptom (what users saw):
- Impact (which version, how many users, for how long):
- How it was found (on-call / user report / monitoring or usage metrics / ourselves):
- {{ pr }} that introduced it: #
- {{ pr }} that fixed it: #    Fixed in version:

## Which layer should have caught it

For each layer, answer "why didn't this layer catch it", and land at least one improvement:

| Layer | What happened there | Improvement ("none" if none) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Before the {{ pr }} (local checks, `/code-review`, `/security-review`) |  |  |
| {{ ai }} review (`{{ instructionsPath }}`, security checklist `{{ securityPath }}`) |  |  |
| Required checks (`.github/workflows/{{ policy.names.ciWorkflow }}`: {{#each ci.requiredChecks}}`{{ this }}`{{#unless @last}}, {{/unless}}{{/each}}{{#if ci.releaseGate}}, `{{ ci.releaseGate.name }}`{{/if}}) |  |  |
| People by risk (was the `{{ policy.names.gateContext }}` tier too low, did the right people look) |  |  |
| Merge queue |  |  |
{{#if stagedRollout}}
| Dogfooding and staged rollout |  |  |
{{else}}
| Dogfooding and staged rollout (not in place yet: would it have limited the impact this time?) |  |  |
{{/if}}
| On-call detection and stopping the damage |  |  |

## Review eval

- [ ] Add the {{ pr }} that introduced the problem to `{{ policy.paths.evalCases }}` (`kind: "bug"`, with the file, the lines and a one-sentence description; the fields are listed in the `README.md` next to it). From then on, the weekly review eval, and every change to the review instructions, checks whether {{ ai }} catches it.
- [ ] If {{ ai }} did flag the problem at the time but the comment was answered with `wrong:` or `wontfix:`, note it here and consider changing the review instructions.
